  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAn MCS is likely to slowly decay through the overnight hours\ntonight as it enters eastern OH plus western PA, with radar\nsignatures showing its outflow beginning to outrace the\nupdrafts. Until it dissipates, gusty wind plus lightning and\nbrief heavy rain can be expected with diminishing instances of\nlocalized damaging winds that could knock over trees/power lines\nand result in power outages.\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms are again expected Saturday,\nespecially in the afternoon and evening, as diurnal\ndestabilization occurs. ML CAPE is progged from 1500-2500 j/kg\nby afternoon, with dry mid level air again resulting in a\ndamaging wind potential. Models also indicate more of a trigger\nwith a weak shortwave/surface boundary across the region. These\nscattered showers/thunderstorms should continue into Saturday\nnight, though the intensity should gradually decrease with\nwaning instability.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected to become likely through\nthe day on Sunday, continuing into Monday, with the approach of\na stronger shortwave trough. The severe weather potential is\nexpected to gradually shift SE'ward each day, with better\ninstability progged to the east. Sunday has the higher potential\nfor severe storms across the Upper Ohio Valley region, with\nbuilding diurnal instability (ML CAPE 1500-2500 j/kg.) There\nwill also be some potential for flooding, especially Sunday into\nMonday, though this will be highly dependent on location and\nextent of convection on prior days. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHot and humid conditions will continue through Saturday. Issued\na Heat Advisory for a portion of the area, in coordination with\nsurrounding NWS offices, this evening through Saturday outside \nof the expanded extreme heat warning. Expect heat indices near \n100 in these areas. Further to the south, the combination of \ntemperatures in the lower to mid 90s and dew points in the lower\n70s will result in heat indices near 105 through Saturday \nafternoon. Decreasing 500 mb heights with the approach of the \nshortwave trough should result in lower temperatures and heat \nindices by Sunday, though conditions will still be warm and \nhumid.",
